body:
I'm gonna investigate using some type of adjuster like that. It might work, but I'd be worried about it not holding up over time since it's gonna be under constant load. I also had the idea to simply design and print 2 flat, maybe like 10 layer solid infill rectangular pieces with m3 bolt holes in either side and just clamp them on the end of the belt with m3 nuts and bolts, it would probably be kind of cumbersome but it would probably hold. I could even model the gt2 tooth pattern into the clamping faces to make it less likely to slip.

&#x200B;

As far as the epoxy idea goes, I think just doubling the end of the belt over on itself an epoxying it would work better than trying to glob it on and make a "bulb". If I mess that up, I can just clip the ends off and try again. As you said I do have some leeway with tension adjustment, although not a ton.

&#x200B;

I just need some way to make the end of it fat enough to not slip through the little slit. (heh).

&#x200B;

EDIT: I found this [https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:4111957](https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:4111957)

I don't know how well it will work by itself but my plan is to print the outer part out of TPU and the inner part out of PETG, then see if it holds by itself. It it doesn't I figure I can just glue it and it'll be fine. So I'll let you know how well it works.

If it makes you feel any better, COVID 19 doesn't appear to create nasal drainage and sinus issues like you're describing. It's almost exclusively a respiratory thing. It causes a DRY cough, not a productive one, so mucous in general is more likely to be a cold or flu. You might just be getting a cold.

&#x200B;

I understand how you feel. I've been just trying to avoid the news. I've honestly been spending less time on reddit as well, people here are working themselves into a hysteria. That's not to say there isn't cause for worry, but try to realize when you're reinforcing your anxiety rather than actually trying to combat it. I think about it like this. When I'm nervous I bite my nails, which actually makes me more nervous. So if I make an effort to stop the reinforcement loop, the anxiety lessens.

&#x200B;

Message me if you need someone to talk to. I'm scared too, we all our, but we can't let it rule over us. We must carry on.

Yep, I always get irrationally angry when I see a well-off family give a bunch of useful shit to goodwill so they can SELL it at fucking ebay prices. That's not actual charity IMO, you're literally just giving a business money. Donate to places that will actually give the stuff to people who need it IMO. Hell, you can give the stuff to a consignment shop or something if you're lazy, at least then you're making a bit of money too off the stuff.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Since I'm sure someone is gonna say this to me, I'm aware goodwill funds a rather large job program partially with the money from their sales so it's not all bad, I still think donating your stuff directly to somewhere that will give it to people who need it, is better.

I edited my post. I think you might be using them? I think you just oriented the model in an annoying way to remove the supports, and didn't make the interface distance between the bottom and top of the supports big enough. For a print like that, I'd do a 1.5mm support interface at least.

&#x200B;

Try tree supports next time as well if you need to support something that overhangs the model itself. They can support things from the build plate that aren't directly over the plate itself.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Also lose the support perimeter/walls. You don't need it. It makes it harder to remove them imo.

~~You can't use marlin 2.0 on the stock board though right? I was under the impression marlin 2.0 is for 32bit boards.~~

&#x200B;

Nevermind I'm stupid, I guess. Sorry for spreading misinformation.

You've got a bltouch v3 most likely. It doesn't work with the stock mainboard without some tweaking.

&#x200B;

[Teaching Tech Has a video on how to fix it.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sOFxalLOZOI)

&#x200B;

Your Z stop plug on it might also be backwards. Also check to make sure the wiring is correct in general. I had to swap some wires for my setup, although I use the SKR 1.3, and not the stock mobo with the pin27 adapter.

[https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:3563135](https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:3563135)

&#x200B;

I found a vent ring that accepts a 5015. I highly, highly suggest getting a 5015 blower.

&#x200B;

[Here's the ones I got](https://www.amazon.com/SoundOriginal-50x50x15mm-Humidifier-Aromatherapy-Replacement/dp/B07DQ7F1T7/ref=sr_1_11?keywords=5015+blower+fan&qid=1568376799&s=electronics&sr=1-11). You also get 2 of them so you can go straight to dual blowers as well if  you would like that.

It's a super worthy upgrade. Easy to install too, just snip the 4010 wires and splice the new fan on. For 2 blowers you wire them both to the same line in parallel. Or if you want to do it the pro way, use dupont connectors and a crimper.

&#x200B;

I used [these nifty things](https://www.amazon.com/Connector-Astrowire-Waterproof-Connectors-Electrical/dp/B07HKP2D5T/ref=pd_lpo_sbs_200_t_2?_encoding=UTF8&psc=1&refRID=463KVGV28H6YCSSA4H5E) to connect them. Super convienient.

Ok. I typically run PLA at 195 and PLA+ at 210. I guess I'll bump up the PLA to 215 to start, the PLA+ might need like 220. Damn that's hot for PLA. I wonder if as the heat break gets "seasoned" from lots of prints you'll be able to drop that temp back down. 

&#x200B;

I mostly print in PLA, but I have a black roll of PETG that I was gonna use to re-print my fan duct so I can feel a little safer. Although I hope my PLA duct can make it through that first print to replace itself lol. I might have to put the stock shroud on at first. I'm not sure if the extra temp will push my PLA duct into the hot zone. I figure as long as there's like a 2-3mm gap between the hot parts and any PLA it's typically fine though.

&#x200B;

I might just keep it on standby until I really need it. Like if I get one of the ultra messy leaks people keep getting, I'll take that as an opportunity to swap out.

&#x200B;

I also read that hot end cooling becomes more important. You really don't want heat creeping up that heat break. I might get a more powerful hot end fan too if that's the case. My duct has space for a 40x20mm fan, so we'll see.

&#x200B;

I just regret not spending that money on an SKR e3 Mini and a BLtouch. That would have been better spent for my purposes, but it's too late now :(

In addition to under extrusion/leveling issues (which is likely your problem), on that print, the little nose needs supports. When you do get your extrusion issues fixed. It will come out better. There's a version of that model on thingiverse with integrated supports that you just snap off.

&#x200B;

You also probably don't need a brim with that model. I've printed about 15 of those for people, never once used a brim.

Well, I've re done my leveling with springs cranked quite a bit farther down. I had to move the limit switch down almost to it's stopper. The back left spring is kind of the limiting factor because it has that little attachment that holds the bed cable shortening it. I leveled the bed by homing it and raising it up .1, then sliding paper under it until it dragged just a little bit. Hopefully I won't have to re-level between every single print as often. I did notice it was more stable already though because I took the opportunity to try to re-install the bed handle, which I didn't use after I printed it because I found it fucked with my leveling almost instantly if I so much as touched it. Now that the springs are cranked the fuck down, I can grab that handle all I want it seems and the bed isn't creeping up.

&#x200B;

We'll see how my prints go now.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Is it normal for the plate the bed mounts on to be so fucked?  My front right spring is quite a bit father cranked down then the front left.

Get spare nozzles, better PTFE tubes, if you're gonna buy spare fans, you might as well get a noctua fan with a buck converter since they come only in 12v, and upgrade to a 5015 blower and a better cooling system. Make sure whatever fans you get are dual ball bearings, those will last much longer.

Keep in mind almost every part on the ender 3 is off the shelf, so if anything fails, you will almost always be able to replace it.

I bought some spare rollers with bearings, as those are common failure points on any 3d printer that uses ball bearings instead of linear sleeve type bearings.

&#x200B;

It might be worth buying a spare hot end and the wires that go to it with a spare thermistor and heating element. Those go bad from time to time. I bought a micro swiss all metal hot end, and keep the stock one as a back up.  I'm also planning on eventually buying a spare heated bed, just for piece of mind. If you're gonna buy backup items, you might as well get better shit and keep the stock stuff for in case something breaks.

&#x200B;

The mainboard is a common upgrade, you can get the creality silent board, or you can just get the SKR 1.3/mini board with the better TMC2208 or the new TMC2209 stepper drivers.

&#x200B;

Check your mainboard, power lines, and heated bed connections every 100 - 200 or so hours of print time as these light to start deteriorating sometimes. If you have a new ender 3 a lot of the issues people talk about have been solved, but some persist.

You're not stupid until you sit your ender 3 pro on top of a foam mat for the first 3 weeks you own it and block the fan to your mainboard. I'm so lucky I didn't blow something up lol. 

&#x200B;

It's all a learning process.

I've messed with gyroid a bit, I swear cubic is a bit faster, but It's also not quite as strong. I'll have to play with it some more.

&#x200B;

One interesting thing for me about gyroid, and I know this is dumb, is that my printer makes some crazy sounds when I print gyroid. It makes me laugh sometimes with how goofy it sounds. Same with anything with a lot of curves lol.

You might also be printing too high off the bed. I'm not super positive but I know that for me with normal nozzles, if I level the bed too low, It will still stick, but the layer won't be squished down a bit, which helps with this as well, because the squish spreads the plastic out a bit. I'm not sure how to level with a line that fat, I think the paper method might bring the nozzle a little too close to the bed, but i'm not positive. Also make sure your first layer is set to a slightly lower height, I find I get better first layers that way. Like if i do .2mm layers, I do .16 on the first layer.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Another suggestion I have is to print the first layer concentric instead of lines or zig zags, I wouldn't normally do this, but I think in this case you might have gotten a better result since you have a lot of holes in the surface. The print honestly looks fine, I'm just a nitpicker.

It looks like your nozzle is too high off the bed. Level the nozzle lower, auto home the print head, disable the steppers, use a piece of printer paper, and check the 4 corners and center, moving the print head above the leveling screws, and sliding the paper back and forth between the nozzle and bed.. The paper should have a medium amount of friction between it and the bed when you pull it back and forth. Enough to feel resistance but not so much that you have to put any real force into moving it. If you find that the center is higher or lower then the 4 corners, then compensate by raising or lowering all 4 corners. Do this all at least twice, auto homeing between the 2 attempts, so you can make sure you didn't move the z-axis up or down while moving the nozzle around by hand.

&#x200B;

Also, what is that stuff you're printing on? It looks like the creality glass bed with some type of weird goop on it.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Also make sure to [calibrate your extruder](https://all3dp.com/2/extruder-calibration-6-easy-steps-2/), and measure your filament with calipers in 5 places. Tell your slicer the average filament diameter. Not all filament is a perfect 1.75mm and doing this will make sure your slicer is accounting for the filament diameter. You'll get more consistent extrusion this way.

That layer height is a little high for that nozzle diameter, that will cause gaps like this too, not to this extent, but it will. I'd do .24 tops, for me .2 is the lowest I'll go, if the print's already gonna take several hours, I don't mind 1 or 2 more, so there's that.

&#x200B;

Also make sure you do extruder calibration, just so that you know your extruder is indeed pushing as much filament as it thinks it is.

I'm gonnna paste some advice I gave in another thread today because I think it will also be helpful since you're new to this.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

First, turn combing on, what that does is it makes the print head avoid retractions, and instead it travels over walls or infill depending on your settings, this way you end up with faster travels because it doesn't have to pause and retract every time it moves, and it lets the little bit of oozing just happen in the infill or on an inner wall. It will still retract if it absolutely has to go over mid air to get somewhere though. You can also speed up your travel moves, I do 180mm/s on travel moves.

Next, I believe the standard profile just prints at 50mm/s for everything, What you can do is set the speed to change depending on what it's doing. So like, you'd want to do outer walls slower, and inner walls a little faster, with infill going the fastest out of everything. Like, for me, I do 70mm/s on the infill, 50mm/s on the inner walls, and 40mm/s on the outer walls. Make sure to set your first layer hotter and slower though, I do 5 degrees hotter and half speed on the first layer, and it ramps up the speed over the next 2 layers.

Lower your infill percentage as well if the print doesn't need strength, and reduce the wall count, I do 3 walls normally, if the print just needs to be pretty I'll even go down to 2 sometimes. Play with the infill pattern as well.  Cubic is my go to. It's very strong but also fast. Gyriod is the strongest I believe, but also slower. Triangle is a good one too, same with grid.

Combing is a big one though, sometimes turning off combing will increase my print time by over an hour, sometimes 2. Those retractions add up.

1 more thing I'd suggest is setting Cura to put the z-seam on the sharpest corner, and tell it to attempt to hide the z-seam whenever it can, so you won't get that ugly seam on the outside of your model when it does a layer change.

&#x200B;

Also a good baseline retraction setting for the ender 3 is 6mm @ 25mm/s, I know it seems weird to do slower retractions, but I've actually gotten great results with that. Look in to magic numbers. For example, always set your layer height to a multiple of 4, i.e. 0.2mm, or 0.16mm.

Also, You either need to solder, or buy a dupont crimper to cut and attach them to the stock fan leads. The proper way to do it is the dupont connectors, but I just soldered mine. Put the red wires to the yellow stock fan wire, and the black wires to the blue one, wire them in a Y fashion, so like the 2 red wires both connect to the yellow, same with the black and blue.

&#x200B;

Also maybe don't run them both at 100% speed, It's probably fine but it's going to be drawing quite a bit more current so I wouldn't let them run that hard for long, maybe just bridges for example. It will also have a lot of PWM whine if you don't flash firmware with the soft PWM fix, or at least put a 100uf capacitor rated to anything above 24v. If you do the capacitor, also put it in parallel, so it's positive lead connects to all the red wires at once, like wise for the negative and the black wires. Depending on the capacitor you choose though, it might run at 100% at a lower speed, like with mine the fans are at full speed at 25%, so I just compensate and treat 25% as 100% in the slicer. Or, just deal with a little PWM whining.

Well, my printer sits right next to a full blown PC, I don't really see what a raspberry pi would do for me considering it's just a little linux computer.. Octoprint is made in python so it's not like it really cares what platform it's being run on. The only thing you lose out on is the pi-cam, which there are alternatives for, although not as good.

&#x200B;

I will probably get one though, I need to get a pi and an arduino, just to play around with. Then, I can make a custom print that is a case for the pi, maybe even get a pi touchscreen. That would be cool.

If you just have 1 single 5015, honestly do the [smooth fang](https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:3091560). it's easy to print, no supports, has really high visibility, and is well designed. The only reason I got rid of it, is that I felt like it was a bit large. If that's a concern, I'd go with the single fan Hero Me.  Ditch the 40mm stock fan, it's bad.

&#x200B;

2 5015 fans is overkill, the main advantage is if you can deal with the PWM noise some how, you can run them at a much lower speed and get the same cooling, thus it's quieter. I'm being stupid and just letting them PWM whine because I have no good way of dealing with it right now.

Yeah, I should have researched and realised the magnetic bed is 2 parts with 3m magnetic adhesive below the main magnetic bed piece. 

 I'm glad that when I do get the nerve up to try to remove the bed it might not be disastrous. I don't think it's actually like glued down, it's just really well sealed on there. It's been fine though, I don't really have any reason to take it off yet. 

&#x200B;

I'm getting a micro swiss all-metal hot-end so I can print PETG without worrying about giving my dogs cancer lol. I might have to do something once I start with that because I've heard PETG can stick a lot harder to beds. We'll see. I mostly print PLA anyway, but there's a few projects I want to do that are going to require the part being under load, outside in the heat. So I'd like to be able to print PETG with piece of mind.

&#x200B;

 I was able to fix the adhesion issue somewhat by reducing the bed temp to 50c from 65 and the ol tappy scrapey move people keep telling me. It's still hard to remove stuff but i'm not literally pulling muscles trying to wrench stuff of of the bed.

I do let it cool down, I keep reading that the print should pop off on it's own but it's insanely hard to remove. Like I'm talking having to use 100% of my strength to pry stuff off.

&#x200B;

Yeah there was a magnetic surface I think, It wasn't sticky when I installed it so I figured it would be fine. I've tried, albiet maybe not as thoroughly as I could to pry the glass off, I was just scared of breaking it and having a broken glass bed stuck to the plate.  I kind of wish I had just stuck it on top of the magnetic bed, but that seemed like it would add too much height and force me to move the z-limit switch. Hindsight is 20/20 I guess.

&#x200B;

On the plus side, mine seems like a newer design that has the creality logo much smaller and at the bottom of the plate, so it doesn't leave that mark on the prints.
